What is an Interface in C#?
An Interface is a contract that defines what methods or properties a class must implement, but generally does not contain their implementation.
Example in .NET Core
1. Create Interface
public interface IEmployeeService
{
    void GetEmployee();
}


2. Implement Interface
public class EmployeeService : IEmployeeService
{
    public void GetEmployee()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Employee Details");
    }
}


3. Register in Program.cs
builder.Services.AddScoped<IEmployeeService, EmployeeService>();


4. Inject into Controller
public class EmployeeController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly IEmployeeService _service;

    public EmployeeController(IEmployeeService service)
    {
        _service = service;
    }
}


Interview-ready answer
“An interface is a contract that defines methods and properties that a class must implement.

In .NET Core, we use interfaces to achieve loose coupling, abstraction, and better testability. For example, we create an IEmployeeService interface and implement it in EmployeeService. Then we inject the interface into the controller using Dependency Injection.

This makes our application easier to maintain and test.”
